# `npm run dev`, `restart` and `stop` — what differs between developing this app and using it.
#
# There is no dev build and no prod build: the app is installed, not deployed, and the only axis
# that ever changes is which library it fetches from (src/library/origin.ts). `npm start` reads
# the published site, which is correct for every real install; this file points a dev run at the
# local one — `xokolat-web`, `node serve.mjs`, port 8080.
#
# ⚠️ COMMITTED ON PURPOSE, against the `.env.*` rule beside it. It holds no secret — it is a
# localhost URL — and the point is that "which library dev reads" is a fact about this repo
# rather than a fact about somebody's shell history. Keys are the opposite call: they live in an
# owner-only file that is never committed and never read back (src/secrets.ts).
#
# A variable already set in the environment still wins, so a one-off is
# `XOKOLAT_LIBRARY=https://xoko.lat npm run dev`.
XOKOLAT_LIBRARY=http://127.0.0.1:8080

# The second thing: dev listens on every interface, an install listens on loopback only
# (src/server/main.ts explains why the default is closed). This is what makes the app reachable
# from another computer you own — a tailnet address like http://<machine>.tailnet.ts.net:18080,
# or a phone on the same wifi. Narrow it to `100.x.y.z` if you'd rather take only the tailnet.
XOKOLAT_HOST=0.0.0.0

# The third and fourth: dev has its own port and its own folders, so a delivered build — the zip,
# `npm start` here, which reads none of this — runs beside it on 18080 with nothing shared. Two
# servers writing one registry and one index is a race, and a test of the zip should meet what a
# new user meets, not this checkout's setup. `~/` is expanded by src/paths.ts.
XOKOLAT_PORT=18081
XOKOLAT_DATA="~/Library/Application Support/xokolat-dev"
XOKOLAT_CONTENT=~/Documents/xokolat-dev
